WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.585
An OSINT workstation is a dedicated environment for legitimate research using lawfully accessible public information.

00:00:07.585 --> 00:00:17.010
It brings together browsing, documentation, evidence preservation, analysis, and reporting while keeping case activity separate from ordinary personal use.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.079
Separation reduces contamination.

00:00:19.079 --> 00:00:27.124
Personal cookies, saved accounts, autofill, bookmarks, and browsing history can confuse research or reveal information unnecessarily.

00:00:27.124 --> 00:00:35.629
A dedicated environment makes it easier to know which settings, artifacts, and files belong to the investigation.

00:00:35.629 --> 00:00:39.536
The workstation should support a traceable workflow.

00:00:39.536 --> 00:00:52.638
Analysts need to record requirements, preserve URLs and timestamps, organize public documents and captures, distinguish raw collection from analysis, and produce reports that another reviewer can understand.

00:00:52.638 --> 00:00:57.925
Privacy and evidence handling matter as much as search capability.

00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:05.511
Case material may contain sensitive personal, organizational, or technical details even when sources are public.

00:01:05.511 --> 00:01:11.717
Access controls, encryption, retention, and controlled sharing protect that material from misuse.

00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:17.004
A workstation does not guarantee anonymity, accuracy, or legal authority.

00:01:17.004 --> 00:01:26.428
A VPN, virtual machine, or separate profile changes technical conditions but does not erase logs, policy, ethics, or accountability.

00:01:26.428 --> 00:01:31.715
The environment must reinforce authorized behavior rather than encourage bypasses.

00:01:31.715 --> 00:01:36.542
The goal is a clean and dependable research system.

00:01:36.542 --> 00:01:52.402
It should help an analyst answer defined questions, preserve source context, test conclusions, and report responsibly without mixing personal activity, collecting unnecessary data, or turning tool experimentation into the purpose of the work.
